===Prior art===

Several earlier BIPs proposed changing Bitcoin's block size limit; all are now Closed:

* [[bip-0100.mediawiki|BIP 100]] (Jeff Garzik): a hard limit set by coinbase vote on the difficulty retargeting schedule.
* [[bip-0101.mediawiki|BIP 101]] (Gavin Andresen): a maximum size that grows over time at a predictable rate.
* [[bip-0102.mediawiki|BIP 102]] (Jeff Garzik): a one-time increase to 2 MB.
* [[bip-0103.mediawiki|BIP 103]] (Pieter Wuille): growth intended to follow technological improvements.
* [[bip-0104.mediawiki|BIP 104]] (t.khan): adjustment targeting 75% full blocks, based on the average size of the previous 2016 blocks.
* [[bip-0105.mediawiki|BIP 105]] (BtcDrak): retargeting every 2016 blocks from miner votes cast in the coinbase, with a difficulty penalty for voting to increase.
* [[bip-0106.mediawiki|BIP 106]] (Upal Chakraborty): a dynamically controlled cap based on previous block sizes and, optionally, fees.
* [[bip-0107.mediawiki|BIP 107]] (Washington Y. Sanchez): a dynamic limit based on transaction volume.
* [[bip-0109.mediawiki|BIP 109]] (Gavin Andresen): a one-time increase to 2,000,000 bytes with sigop and sighash limits.

This proposal differs from BIP 104 and BIP 106 by using medians over long windows rather than the average of a single retarget period, and from BIP 100 and BIP 105 by not requiring explicit votes.

Outside Bitcoin:

* Bitcoin Cash's CHIP-2023-04, the Adaptive Blocksize Limit Algorithm (ABLA; status: Accepted), adjusts the limit after each block from an exponentially weighted moving average of block sizes plus an elastic buffer, with a 32 MB floor.<ref>bitcoincashautist, [https://gitlab.com/0353F40E/ebaa CHIP-2023-04 Adaptive Blocksize Limit Algorithm for Bitcoin Cash].</ref> The CHIP's discussion thread includes back-tests comparing it to single- and dual-median rules.<ref>[https://bitcoincashresearch.org/t/chip-2023-04-adaptive-blocksize-limit-algorithm-for-bitcoin-cash/1037 Bitcoin Cash Research forum thread for CHIP-2023-04].</ref>
* Monero uses a dynamic block weight with a short-term median over 100 blocks, a long-term median over 100,000 blocks and a block reward penalty for blocks above the effective median, with a maximum block weight of twice the effective median.<ref>Cuprate, [https://monero-book.cuprate.org/consensus_rules/blocks/weights.html The Monero Book: Weights].</ref> Unlike Monero, this proposal does not use a reward penalty.

===Concerns to be addressed===

The following concerns apply to any block size increase and are not resolved by this draft:

* '''Full node resource requirements.''' A higher limit can increase bandwidth, storage, initial block download time and UTXO set growth. With ''M'' = 10 the limit can be up to ten times the median block weight.
* '''Validation cost.''' Larger blocks permit more expensive worst-case blocks, including legacy signature hashing costs. [[bip-0054.md|BIP 54]] (Consensus Cleanup) addresses some worst-case validation costs; whether this proposal should require it is an open question.
* '''Propagation and centralization.''' Larger blocks take longer to propagate, which can favour larger or better-connected miners.
* '''Chain split.''' As with any hard fork, activation without near-universal adoption results in two chains.

===Open questions===

# Activation mechanism and height ''H<sub>A</sub>''.
# Value of the floor ''F'' (this draft uses the current 4,000,000 weight units).
# Value of the cap ''C''.
# Whether ''M'' = 10 is appropriate for Bitcoin.
# Whether the windows include blocks mined before activation (this draft: yes).
# Whether this proposal should require [[bip-0054.md|BIP 54]] or other worst-case validation limits.

==Backward Compatibility==

This proposal is a hard fork. Nodes that do not implement it will reject any block with weight above 4,000,000 or sigop cost above 80,000, and will follow a different chain from that block onward. All nodes, including non-mining nodes, wallets and services that validate blocks, must upgrade before activation to follow the upgraded chain.

As long as no block exceeds the current limits, upgraded and non-upgraded nodes accept the same blocks.

Implementations also need to review limits that are not consensus rules but are tied to the current maximum block size, for example the maximum peer-to-peer message length in Bitcoin Core (4,000,000 bytes), which would otherwise prevent relaying blocks larger than that.<ref>Bitcoin Core <code>MAX_PROTOCOL_MESSAGE_LENGTH = 4 * 1000 * 1000</code>, [https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/9320233a3d589eec0a25695eebe92bd35e0814c4/src/net.h#L66 src/net.h line 66]; <code>MAX_BLOCK_SERIALIZED_SIZE</code>, <code>MAX_BLOCK_WEIGHT</code> and <code>MAX_BLOCK_SIGOPS_COST</code> in [https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/9320233a3d589eec0a25695eebe92bd35e0814c4/src/consensus/consensus.h#L13-17 src/consensus/consensus.h lines 13–17].</ref> The <code>getblocktemplate</code> fields <code>sizelimit</code>, <code>weightlimit</code> and <code>sigoplimit</code> ([[bip-0145.mediawiki|BIP 145]]) would need to report values derived from ''LIMIT(h)''.

==Reference Implementation==

There is no Bitcoin reference implementation yet. '''TODO.'''

The algorithm this proposal adapts is implemented in the Nexa full node (MIT license) in [https://gitlab.com/nexa/nexa/-/blob/df4ff0e5ba34882d53601398eff49710bc2a78a6/src/consensus/adaptive_blocksize.cpp src/consensus/adaptive_blocksize.cpp]. A non-consensus Python model of the rule in this document, which produced the test vectors below, is:

<source lang="python">
def median_odd(values):
    assert len(values) % 2 == 1
    return sorted(values)[(len(values) - 1) // 2]

def next_max_block_weight(weights, S, L, M, F, C):
    # weights: weights of all blocks up to and including the parent, oldest first
    m = 0
    if len(weights) >= L + 1:
        m = max(median_odd(weights[-(S + 1):]), median_odd(weights[-(L + 1):]))
    elif len(weights) >= S + 1:
        m = median_odd(weights[-(S + 1):])
    return min(max(m * M, F), C)

def max_sigop_cost(limit, R=50):
    return limit // R
</source>

